"""
Problem:
--------
Given an integer array, return the kth largest element.

Example:
    nums = [3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4]
    k = 2

    Sorted:
        [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

    2nd largest = 5

Key Idea:
---------
Use Quickselect instead of fully sorting the array.

Convert "kth largest" into an index in ascending order:

    target = len(nums) - k

Example:
    nums = [1,2,3,4,5,6]
    k = 2

    target = 6 - 2 = 4

So we need the element at index 4 → 5.

3-Way Partition:
----------------
Choose a pivot and divide the current range into:

    smaller | equal | greater

Using:
    low → first equal position
    i   → current element
    high → last equal position

After partition:

    [left ... low-1]   → smaller
    [low ... high]     → equal
    [high+1 ... right] → greater

Then check where `target` lies.

If:
    target < low
        → search smaller section

If:
    target > high
        → search greater section

Otherwise:
    target is inside the equal section
        → return nums[target]

Why 3-way partition?
--------------------
It handles duplicate values efficiently by grouping all values
equal to the pivot together.

Important:
----------
When nums[i] > pivot:

    swap(nums[i], nums[high])
    high -= 1

We do NOT increase `i` here because the new value moved from
`high` still needs to be checked.

Complexity:
-----------
Average Time: O(n)
Worst Time:   O(n²)
Space:        O(1)

Memory Trick:
-------------
    kth largest
        ↓
    target = n - k
        ↓
    Partition:
    SMALL | EQUAL | GREATER
        ↓
    Find target section
"""
    

class Solution:
    def findKthLargest(self, nums, k):

        # Convert kth largest into ascending-order index
        target = len(nums) - k

        # Start of the search range
        left = 0

        # End of the search range
        right = len(nums) - 1

        # Continue until target is found
        while left <= right:

            # Choose middle element as pivot
            pivot = nums[(left + right) // 2]

            # 3-way partition pointers
            low = left
            i = left
            high = right

            # Partition the current range
            while i <= high:

                # Current value is smaller than pivot
                if nums[i] < pivot:

                    # Move smaller value to the left
                    nums[low], nums[i] = nums[i], nums[low]

                    # Move both pointers forward
                    low += 1
                    i += 1

                # Current value is greater than pivot
                elif nums[i] > pivot:

                    # Move greater value to the right
                    nums[i], nums[high] = nums[high], nums[i]

                    # Shrink the right section
                    high -= 1

                    # Do NOT increment i
                    # New nums[i] still needs to be checked

                # Current value equals pivot
                else:

                    # Keep it in the equal section
                    i += 1

            # Smaller | Equal | Greater
            # [left ... low-1]
            # [low ... high]
            # [high+1 ... right]

            # Target is in the smaller section
            if target < low:
                right = low - 1

            # Target is in the greater section
            elif target > high:
                left = high + 1

            # Target is inside the equal section
            else:
                return nums[target]
